## What the public package does NOT measure

Stated plainly, with where the gap closes when it closes:

1. **Browser journeys beyond one isolated host comparison.** The machinery is
   committed (`reference-harness/browser/`), the 51 journeys are defined as
   data (`reference-harness/browser/journeys/synthetic-v1.json`), and a
   comparison run is committed: on 2026-11-02 all 51 journeys, signed-in and
   anonymous, matched a live Ruby 3.2.3 server running stock Decko 0.20.0 /
   card 1.110.0 on completion, addresses, text, structure, form fields, mail,
   database rows and editors, with the strict-parity switches at their
   defaults and the test settings the harness's port launcher applies
   ([handover/EVIDENCE.md](handover/EVIDENCE.md), section 2;
   [runs/signed-in-default-51-20261102](runs/signed-in-default-51-20261102/README.md)).
   Not measured: that comparison on any tree later than the one it ran on
   (`f174eac2`), the per-journey raw observations (not committed), and a
   deployed site — the checker states its own scope as an isolated host
   comparison, not acceptance of a deployed site.
2. **PBKDF2 storage of a newly set password, in the harness grade.** The
   default PBKDF2 policy is graded by its own adapter configuration,
   `reference-harness/adapters/ts-pbkdf2/`, which runs the full synthetic-v1
   grade with PBKDF2 on and declares one divergence to the grader
   (`--declared-divergences`, `reference-harness/runner/divergences.mjs`),
   one column wide: `cards.db_content` of a `+*password` card. Every other
   byte is compared. Stated plainly, the exception hides one behavioural
   difference: Ruby writes nothing at sign-in, while the port rewrites that
   digest; the grade establishes that the rewrite changes the digest and
   nothing else.
   Reproduce it with `node reference-harness/runner/run.mjs --adapter
   reference-harness/adapters/ts-pbkdf2/adapter.json --fixture
   reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1 --out tmp/grade-pbkdf2-results`
   and then `node reference-harness/runner/grade.mjs --fixture
   reference-harness/fixtures/synthetic-v1 --actual tmp/grade-pbkdf2-results
   --declared-divergences
   reference-harness/adapters/ts-pbkdf2/declared-divergences.json`; the
   report from 2026-10-14 (commit 77c4423d) is
   `reference-harness/grade-reports/synthetic-v1-pbkdf2-report.json`. No
   synthetic-v1 scenario sets a password, so storing a new password as
   PBKDF2 is not graded. If one did, it would fail: the `card_changes`
   history row holds the digest too, and that column is not masked. It is
   covered by unit tests (`tests/card/auth/password-hash.test.ts`,
   `tests/card/auth/signin.test.ts`), not by the harness grade.
3. **Hosted D1 / Bunny write semantics.** The transaction and atomic-save
   suites ran against workerd's local D1 under Miniflare and a Hrana v2 test
   server backed by real SQLite, and against two hosted services on
   2026-10-14 and 2026-10-15 ([decisions/0007](decisions/0007-atomic-saves-and-transaction-contract.md),
   adapter table; [hosted-conformance.md](hosted-conformance.md), "Hosted
   results" and "Cloudflare D1 run, 2026-10-15"; their result records are
   kept outside this repository):
   - **Hosted D1.** On 2026-10-14, through a remote binding, the 7
     transaction conformance cases and 10 save scenarios passed, in a run
     that predates waiting reads. On 2026-10-15, through the runner on a
     scratch database, from commit `85dd897d` (not the integration head):
     transaction conformance and save scenarios 17 of 17, atomic saves 11 of
     11 and multi-user read isolation 2 of 2 passed, and **concurrent saves
     failed 2 of 3** — in both failing cases the save issued first was the
     one refused, the opposite of what the test expects, and the cause was
     not recorded. That run has not been repeated.
   - **Hosted Bunny Database.** On 2026-10-14, a scratch database passed
     transaction conformance and save scenarios 17 of 17 and atomic saves 11
     of 11; concurrent saves failed 1 of 3 in the first run and passed 3 of 3
     in a second run after a driver change, and multi-user read isolation
     failed 1 of 2 in both runs with Bunny's `TRANSACTION_TIMEOUT`. A
     2026-10-15 run from `85dd897d` passed all four suites.

   Never run against sqld or Turso. No committed evidence describes a
   production write on any host, and no hosted-provider acceptance is
   established
   ([handover/EVIDENCE.md](handover/EVIDENCE.md), "What these results do not
   cover"). The intermediate-visibility
   window on D1 is closed: a reader on another connection waits for a save
   in progress instead of reading its rows, verified under Miniflare by
   `tests/card/actions/d1-multi-user-saves.test.ts`. The runner that checks
   the same suites against hosted D1, Turso and Bunny is
   `tools/hosted-conformance/run.mjs` ([hosted-conformance.md](hosted-conformance.md)).
4. **Mod views outside the byte grade.** Every mod Decko 0.20.0 ships is
   ported to TypeScript; the compatibility report's behavioural verdict grades
   378 of 565 declared views byte-identical to Ruby's captured answer, and a
   ported view outside that set is unproven against Ruby's bytes
   (`tools/compat-report/mod-compat-report.md`). A third-party mod written in
   Ruby for upstream Decko must be ported to TypeScript before use, because
   the port does not run Ruby
   ([decisions/0004](decisions/0004-mods-compiled-into-the-view-registry.md)).
5. **The mail a delayed job sends.** card-mod-delayed_job is ported
   (`src/card/delayed-job/`, [delayed-jobs.md](delayed-jobs.md)):
   `integrate_with_delay` events become `delayed_jobs` rows worked off after
   the response on the two hosts that wire the queue, the Node host
   (`vm-staging-server.mjs`, when `DECKO_DELAYING=true`) and the Cloudflare
   Worker (`worker.ts`; the shipped `wrangler.toml` sets
   `DECKO_DELAYING = "true"` and binds the `decko-ts-delayed-jobs` queue).
   No Bunny host wires the queue, so on Bunny those events run inside the
   request. With delaying off, the mod's default, they run in line as Ruby
   runs them. The row and its handler document are checked against a Ruby
   capture (`tests/card/delayed-job/delayed-job.test.ts`), and the default
   configuration by `tests/exercise/deploy-10.test.ts`. Not measured: the
   mail a job sends when the worker runs it, compared with Ruby's, and a
   hosted deployment with delaying on.
6. **Anything a skipped probe would have covered** (see Persistence above):
   pod round-trips and the production-deck runs of the write batteries on the
   real oracle deck. The 70-step write sequence and the 90-step scenario
   battery are public on the committed `write-battery-v1` synthetic deck,
   apart from the steps withheld until disclosure (above). What remains
   non-public is the same measurement on a copy of a real production deck.

