## What changed from the original proposal

The proposal upstream ([decko-commons/decko#1282](https://github.com/decko-commons/decko/issues/1282))
promised five things that later measurement moved. Each is listed here with
the section of this page that says where the promise actually stands, so a
claim quoted anywhere can be checked against the instrument behind it.

| The proposal promised | What stands here | Check it in |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Hosting is effectively free ("$0 on Cloudflare's free tier") | Withdrawn as a total-cost claim. This repository holds **no cost measurements** — the Bunny deploy document says so in terms — and the page-view cost model built alongside it prices anonymous read-only views from a private deployment with provider prices read on a stated date. Cloudflare's free plan does not fit a real deck. | [deploy-bunny.md](deploy-bunny.md); [deploy-cloudflare.md](deploy-cloudflare.md) |
| A constant number of queries regardless of depth | **Met where the stored nest index describes the content.** A cold render reads the root's whole nest closure from `card_references` (ref_type `I`, kept by the write path) in one prelude statement and costs three round trips at every depth 1 through 20 (measured 2026-10-14, `DEPTH_SWEEP_WRITE_ARTIFACT=1 npx vitest run tests/card/render/depth-sweep.test.ts`, which writes its receipt to `benchmarks/results/depth-sweep-ts.json`; the receipt is generated, not shipped). Content the index does not describe falls back to three round trips plus one per nesting level below the root, however many cards each level holds. `tests/card/render/depth-sweep.test.ts` asserts both at every depth; `tests/card/render/deep-nesting-trip-attribution.test.ts` measures the fallback. | [scaling-comparison-deep-nesting.md](scaling-comparison-deep-nesting.md) |
| Same data, no migration required | **Withdrawn.** Import reads a SQLite deck file, a `mysqldump` text dump or deck-INSERT SQL. A separate importer loads a Ruby Decko PostgreSQL database dumped with `pg_dump` into an empty PostgreSQL database after creating the port's own schema there; it is tested on synthetic input only and is not qualified. The PostgreSQL adapter (`PostgresDriver`, `src/server/drivers/postgres.ts`) is in the tree and passed the runtime database matrix 5 of 5 against PostgreSQL 16.15 on 2026-11-02 — an engine-level test on the port's own schema, with no Ruby comparison. Not established: the port running on an existing Decko PostgreSQL deck, in Ruby's schema, without that import, and any migration onto PostgreSQL or MySQL. While Ruby still serves the deck this engine must store Ruby's own password digest, because Ruby cannot verify PBKDF2; D1's transaction semantics differ from Ruby's and SQLite's. | `tools/migrate/IMPORT_DECK_RECIPE.md`; [postgres.md](postgres.md); [handover/EVIDENCE.md](handover/EVIDENCE.md), section 3 and "What these results do not cover"; [handover/MIGRATION.md](handover/MIGRATION.md); [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) "Migration compatibility at a glance" |
| Faster responses at the edge | **Not established.** No speed run is recorded against a release candidate, and no response-time comparison is committed evidence anywhere in this repository. The published, reproducible comparison is round trips per render. | [scaling-comparison-deep-nesting.md](scaling-comparison-deep-nesting.md) |
| One click provisions everything | **Not verified.** The `wrangler.toml` the button ships asks for one Worker, one D1 database (`DB`), one R2 bucket (`FILES`), two queues, each with a producer and a consumer (`DELAYED_JOBS` on `decko-ts-delayed-jobs` for delayed jobs, `DELAYED` on `decko-ts-delayed` for the periodic sweep) and a daily cron trigger. It holds no KV namespace and no secrets. Its three `[vars]` are plain non-secret values, all on: `DECKO_WRITE_PATH = "1"` (a writable deck; `"0"` makes every card write answer `403`), `DECKO_FIRST_RUN = "1"` (the token-gated `/setup` window) and `DECKO_DELAYING = "true"`. `npm run deploy` applies the migrations in `deploy/migrations/` (schema, a small demo deck and its account system) and issues the one-time setup link. `tools/deploy/rehearse-deploy-button.mjs` rehearses the bindings offline with wrangler's local runtime; that is not a deploy through the button on a Cloudflare account. | [deploy-cloudflare.md](deploy-cloudflare.md); README, "Deploy to Cloudflare" |

Two further promises are covered by the sections above rather than by an
instrument: the mod story is that every mod Decko ships is ported, graded by
the compatibility report's declaration labels and behavioural verdict (item 4
above), and "exact output parity" is the
grade of record at the top of this page, with what it leaves out stated in
the same table.

